## Coverage contract

| Area | Intended behaviour | Verification needed |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Native shell, sidebar and toolbars | Consistent theme variables and active-theme identity | Fresh load, narrow view, keyboard navigation |
| Standard cards and controls | Shared type, border, focus and semantic colours | Actual card types used by the household |
| Profile palette settings | Theme colour select next to native settings | Home Assistant profile structure for each supported release |
| Supported native Energy graphs | ONS-based chart structure and View data table, with native values and interaction preserved | Fresh Energy entry, dates/comparisons, legends, keyboard chart controls, table values and cleanup |
| History and other charts | Native behaviour and available colour tokens; no Energy adapter coverage | Fresh entry and native data access |
| Native Map | Preserve map attribution, controls, markers and navigation | Actual map provider and marker contrast |
| Custom cards | Use public variables where available | Each installed card's private styles and updates |
| Three known household cards | Scoped presentation for appliances, waste collections and gas safety | Their actual versions, responsive layouts and native actions |
| civica action links | Native local links and existing fragment navigation | Each configured destination and pop-up |
| Authentication and companion apps | Native branding and authentication remain outside module coverage | Separate native application work would be required |

## Maintained custom cards and legacy configurations

The maintained custom-card types are `custom:civica-house-lighting-card`, `custom:civica-theme-settings` and `custom:civica-action-links`. `custom:govuk-house-lighting-card` remains a registered subclass of the lighting card with the same behaviour, and its existing bundle filename stays available. Saved dashboards using that name continue to load; the alias is not an additional component. The [compatibility register](../home-assistant/compatibility-register.html) separates these components, exact adapters, token-only surfaces and reference specifications.

## Private component adapters

The module targets known component types through their open component roots:

| Host | Presentation adapter |
| --- | --- |
| `ha-pick-theme-row` | Place the colour settings alongside native preferences |
| `ha-sidebar` | Supplied brand identity, masthead icon contrast and numeric attention badges |
| `hass-subpage` | Toolbar icon and focus contrast |
| `ha-top-app-bar-fixed` | Toolbar icon and focus contrast |
| `pa-appliance-card` | Shared type, quiet grouping, button hierarchy and responsive appliance details |
| `home-next-collection-card` | Shared type, collection layout, details and calendar action |
| `pa-gas-safety-card` | Shared type, record layout, details and actions |
| `ha-panel-energy` → supported native graph card → `ha-chart-base` | ONS-based chart structure on the existing ECharts instance and a read-only data table; see the exact [chart support list](../components/charts.html) |

The three household-card adapters use adopted stylesheets, scoped to those exact hosts. They survive ordinary card content updates and are removed when civica is inactive. They change presentation only: device permissions, services, dates, records and action handlers remain with the existing card. Other third-party cards are not covered by these private selectors.

Startup, navigation and theme changes trigger a bounded search for these hosts. The module watches the document's theme style attribute and the sidebar's own badge text. The sidebar observer updates only the presentation and accessible description of numeric attention counts. It does not observe device state or create, clear or acknowledge notifications.

The Energy adapter observes only the mounted Energy panel's component roots and chart lifecycle. It tracks renderer replacement and resizing, updates presentation after native rendering and restores only values it still owns. It does not wrap the chart engine's methods, poll sensors, fetch statistics or change the native source arrays. Unknown chart types retain native presentation.

These adapters depend on the Home Assistant version. If an expected component is absent, the module leaves the native interface usable and you can use the [standalone settings card](colour-settings.html). After each upgrade, check the three household cards and the native host structures.

Safe and recovery modes disable colour controls and restore native branding. Repeated module imports must not add duplicate controls or listeners.

Avoid monkey-patching browser prototypes, replacing native controls wholesale or assuming a selector is a public Home Assistant API. Document each adapter and test its cleanup when the user switches away from civica.

## CSS cannot correct everything

Styling does not fix an upstream missing label, an inaccessible chart tooltip, a closed component root, third-party hardcoded colours or the wording inside an integration's form. Track those as specific compatibility issues with reproduction steps. Do not call them compliant just because surrounding colours match.

## Verification status

Local build, contrast and component tests can be reproduced from the repository. A local preview is not proof of a live Home Assistant deployment. Record the Home Assistant version, browser, components tested and any remaining failures for each release. Do not say a live system has been updated until you have checked that deployment.

Follow [Test the interface](../accessibility/testing.html) for native panels and cold loads.

## Household identity transition

civica identifies the project and its documentation. It is not required as your home's identity.

The existing companion still replaces supported sidebar titles and browser and app icons with civica artwork. It does not yet provide the proposed family-name or custom-crest settings, and this documentation does not change any installation. Do not describe the personalisation examples as available global theme settings. [Personalise your home](personalisation.html) separates current native dashboard titles and picture cards from that proposed behaviour.
